.TH HD-DESKTOP 7 "October 2026" "hd-desktop" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-desktop \- the HyprDebian desktop
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-desktop
depends on the packages of the HyprDebian desktop, so that they are installed
with it and stay installed: Hyprland with uwsm, hyprpaper, hypridle,
hyprlock, its dialogs and XWayland, greetd, the desktop portals of Hyprland
and GTK, kitty, mako, PipeWire with wiremix, its PulseAudio server and rtkit,
the screenshot tools grim, slurp and swappy, the dark GTK theme, qt6ct and
qt6-wayland for the Qt applications, the colour emoji of
fonts-noto-color-emoji, plymouth,
yazi with ouch for its archives, and the applications audacious of
.BR hd-audacious (7),
mpv, imv and firefox.
HyprDebian installs no recommends, so the package depends on everything the
desktop needs.
Versions before 1.27 also depended on git, git-delta, meld, nwg-look,
pulseaudio-utils and the Qt themes; after an upgrade
.B apt autoremove
removes them, so mark the ones that are still used as installed by hand
first, such as
.BR "apt-mark manual git" .
.PP
Its version is the version of HyprDebian, as of
.BR hd-base (7),
and it depends on
.B hd-base
of the same version, so the desktop and the base system are always of the
same release.
.PP
It holds the parts of the desktop, each with a man page of its own:
.TP
.BR hd-art (7)
The wallpapers, in
.IR /usr/share/backgrounds/hyprdebian .
.TP
.BR hd-fonts (7)
JetBrains Mono with the icons of Nerd Fonts.
.TP
.BR hd-menu (1)
The keyboard-driven menu of the key bindings and the application launcher.
.TP
.BR hd-message (1)
The message box: a title, a text and a row of buttons.
.TP
.BR hd-theme (7)
The theme engine: the themes of the system, and
.BR hd-theme (1),
which selects them and applies them to the desktop programs of a user.
.TP
.BR hd-screensaver (7)
The screensaver, which hypridle starts after 2 minutes.
.TP
.BR hd-plymouth (7)
The boot splash, which looks like the lock screen of hyprlock and asks for
the password of an encrypted ZFS dataset.
.PP
These were the packages
.BR hd-art ,
.BR hd-fonts ,
.BR hd-menu ,
.BR hd-theme ,
.B hd-screensaver
and
.B hd-plymouth
before.
.B hd-desktop
conflicts with them, replaces and provides them, so an upgrade removes them;
the themes, the selected theme and the theme files of the users stay.
.SH BOOTING INTO HYPRLAND
greetd logs a user in to Hyprland on tty1 at the boot, without a password,
with
.BR "uwsm start hyprland.desktop" .
The output of Hyprland goes to
.IR /var/log/hyprland.log .
greetd does this once per boot: after a logout, it starts the console on
tty1 and stops.
.PP
The session runs on tty1, the VT of the boot splash, in the place of
.BR getty@tty1.service ,
so the splash stays on the screen until Hyprland has it; see
.BR hd-plymouth (7).
greetd leaves the VT alone, as its config has
.BR "vt = \(dqnone\(dq" ;
.B greetd.service
gives the session tty1 with
.BR XDG_VTNR .
.PP
The config of greetd,
.IR /etc/greetd/config.toml ,
is written by
.BR hd-desktop-render (8),
on the installation and before every start of greetd.
The one of the package greetd is diverted to
.IR /etc/greetd/config.toml.greetd .
The user is the setting
.B user
of the key
.B hd-desktop
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ;
without it, the first user that was made.
.SH FILES FOR NEW USERS
The package installs the files that new users get in their home directory,
in
.IR /etc/skel :
the configs of Hyprland
.RI ( ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.lua ),
hypridle, hyprlock, hyprpaper, hyprtoolkit, kitty, mako, mpv, alsa,
PipeWire, qt5ct, qt6ct, GTK 3, GTK 4 and VSCodium, the PAM config of
hyprlock, and the directories
.I ~/.icons
and
.IR ~/Pictures/Screenshots .
The configs of Hyprland, hyprlock, hyprpaper, hyprtoolkit, kitty, mako,
qt5ct, qt6ct, GTK 3, GTK 4 and VSCodium take the
HyprDebian theme from the theme files that
.B hd-theme \-\-apply
writes next to them; the user runs it after a theme is selected.
The installation of the package copies the files of
.I /etc/skel
that are missing from the home directory of the user who is logged in to
Hyprland, as that user; files that the user has are kept.
It does so on the first installation, and on an upgrade from a version
before 0.3, not on later upgrades.
Other users copy the files they are missing with:
.PP
.RS
cp \-a \-\-update=none /etc/skel/. ~/
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
The key bindings of Hyprland run these commands, in
.IR /usr/bin :
.TP
.BR hd-ask-reboot ,\  hd-ask-poweroff
Ask with
.BR hd-menu (1)
whether to reboot or power off, then run
.B hd-reboot
or
.B hd-poweroff
of
.BR hd-base (7).
.TP
.B hd-mode-select
Choose the resolution and refresh rate of a monitor with
.BR hd-menu (1).
.TP
.BR hd-mount ,\  hd-unmount
Mount a drive or an
.I /etc/fstab
entry, on
.IR /mnt/<user>/<n> ,
and unmount it again, with
.BR hd-menu (1).
They run
.I /usr/libexec/hd-desktop/hd-mount-helper
with sudo; the users in the group
.B sudo
may do so without a password, by
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/hd-desktop .
.TP
.BR hd-volume-up ,\  hd-volume-down ,\  hd-volume-mute
Change the volume of the default output and show it in a notification.
.SS Reboot and power off
In the desktop session,
.BR hd-reboot (1)
and
.BR hd-poweroff (1)
run
.I /usr/libexec/hd-desktop/hd-shutdown
with
.B reboot
or
.BR poweroff .
It asks every window to close, as
.B SUPER + W
does, and runs
.B systemctl reboot
or
.B systemctl poweroff
once all are gone.
When windows of applications that ask something first, such as whether to
save a file, have stayed the same for 3 seconds,
.BR hd-message (1)
names their applications, by window class, process name, pid and window
titles, and offers:
.TP
.B Wait
Close the message and answer the applications; the shutdown goes on once
their windows are gone. Escape does the same.
.TP
.B Kill
End their processes with SIGTERM, and those still running 3 seconds later
with SIGKILL; their unsaved work is lost.
.TP
.B Cancel
Stop the shutdown; the applications that have closed stay closed.
.PP
After
.BR Wait ,
the message comes back when an application closes the windows of its
question but stays open, as when the question was cancelled, or when
.B hd-reboot
or
.B hd-poweroff
runs again.
.B hd-shutdown
works in the transient user unit
.BR hd-shutdown.service ,
whose output is in the journal; a second
.B hd-shutdown
sends it SIGUSR1, which shows the message again.
.SS Pending reboot
The user unit
.B hd-reboot-notify.path
starts
.B hd-reboot-notify.service
in the desktop session when
.I /run/reboot-required
exists: when an upgrade writes it, and at every login until the reboot. It
runs
.IR /usr/libexec/hd-desktop/hd-reboot-notify ,
which shows a notification with the reasons of
.BR hd-reboot-check (1).
It shows once per session. The postinst enables the unit for every user on
its first installation; to switch it off for a user:
.PP
.RS
.nf
systemctl \-\-user disable \-\-now hd\-reboot\-notify.path
.fi
.RE
.PP
The menus above and the application launcher use
.BR hd-menu (1),
which has a lock of its own, so a second press of a key binding opens no
second menu.
.SH APPLICATION LAUNCHER
.BR hd-launcher-cache (1)
writes the menu of the application launcher, the input of
.BR hd-menu (1),
in
.I /var/cache/hd-launcher
for root and in
.I ~/.cache/hd-launcher
for other users, from the
.I .desktop
files in
.IR /usr/share/applications ,
sorted on their names, with their icons; SVG icons are rendered to PNGs of
the
.B icon_size
of the theme.
The key
.B hd-launcher
of the configuration of the user,
.IR ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json ,
or else of the system,
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
leaves applications out and adds items with a command of their own.
The apt hook
.I /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/90hd-launcher
writes the menus of the system and of every user in the group
.B users
again each time apt has run dpkg, with
.BR "hd-launcher-cache \-\-update\-all" ,
and the installation of the package writes them too; a user can write their
own at any time with
.BR "hd-launcher-cache \-\-update" .
.PP
.B Super+Space
opens the menu that
.B hd-launcher-cache
prints, of the user, else of the system, in
.BR hd-menu (1),
and starts the application or item chosen with
.BR "uwsm app \-\-" ,
as a systemd user unit of its own.
The other applications of the key bindings, such as the terminal, the
browser and VSCodium, are started with
.B uwsm app
too; the menus and short commands are not.
.SH DARK THEME
The GTK and the Qt applications and the dialogs of Hyprland look the same:
dark, in the colours of the HyprDebian theme, in its font, and with the file
chooser of GTK.
Their base is the dark Adwaita theme in JetBrainsMono NFM Medium 11;
.B hd-theme \-\-apply
puts the colours of the theme over it, and for GTK the font of the theme in
its size
.BR font-size-small .
.PP
.I /etc/dconf/db/local.d/00-dark-theme
asks GTK applications for the dark Adwaita theme, and
.I 01-font
for the font of HyprDebian, of the interface, the documents and the
monospaced text.
.I /etc/dconf/profile/user
adds that system database to the one of the user, and the installation
compiles it with
.BR "dconf update" .
GTK applications on XWayland have no XSETTINGS and do not read dconf; they
get the same from
.I /etc/gtk-3.0/settings.ini
and
.IR /etc/gtk-4.0/settings.ini .
.PP
The Qt applications get it from qt5ct and qt6ct, which
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=qt5ct
in
.I ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.lua
loads for Qt 5 and Qt 6 alike:
.I ~/.config/qt5ct/qt5ct.conf
and
.I ~/.config/qt6ct/qt6ct.conf
give them the style Fusion with the colour scheme of the theme,
.I ~/.config/qt5ct/colors/hd-theme.conf
and
.IR ~/.config/qt6ct/colors/hd-theme.conf ,
which
.B hd-theme \-\-apply
writes, the font, the Adwaita icons and the file chooser of the desktop portals.
.B qt5ct
and
.B qt6ct
change them.
The package depends on qt6ct only, as the desktop has no Qt 5
applications; for those, install
.BR qt5ct ,
.B qt5-xdgdesktopportal-platformtheme
and
.BR qtwayland5 .
.B qt6-wayland
lets the Qt 6 applications run on Wayland instead of XWayland, which
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland;xcb
asks for first.
The colour scheme hyprdebian in
.I /usr/share/qt5ct/colors
and
.I /usr/share/qt6ct/colors
has the colours of Adwaita-dark.
The font of the Qt applications stays the one in qt5ct.conf and qt6ct.conf.
.PP
The GTK applications get the colours of the theme from
.I ~/.config/gtk-3.0/gtk.css
and
.IR ~/.config/gtk-4.0/gtk.css ,
which import the hd-theme.css that
.B hd-theme \-\-apply
writes next to them: the named colours of Adwaita and libadwaita.
The parts that Adwaita-dark of GTK 3 draws in colours of its own keep them.
The font of the theme goes in the dconf database of the user.
.PP
The dialogs of Hyprland, of hyprland-guiutils, get the same colours, font
and radius from
.IR ~/.config/hypr/hyprtoolkit.conf ,
which sources hd-theme-hyprtoolkit.conf of
.BR "hd-theme \-\-apply" .
An upgrade from a version before 1.28 gives the user the settings of qt5ct,
qt6ct and hyprtoolkit and says to set
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME
to qt5ct in their config of Hyprland, which still names gtk3, and to remove
.BR QT_STYLE_OVERRIDE .
An upgrade from a version before 1.56 gives the user the two files gtk.css,
and says how to give the theme to their settings of qt5ct, qt6ct and
hyprtoolkit, as in
.IR /etc/skel .
.SH REMOVAL
Removing the package puts back the config of the package greetd, which
logs nobody in.
The files in
.IR /etc/skel ,
the sudoers rule, the apt hook and the menu of the launcher stay until the
package is purged; the apt hook does nothing without
.BR hd-launcher-cache .
The menus of the users stay in their home directories.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/greetd/config.toml
The greetd config, written by
.BR hd-desktop-render (8).
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The settings, in the keys
.BR hd-desktop ,
.B hd-launcher
and
.BR hd-display ,
the scale of the theme on the monitor, see
.BR hd-theme (7).
.TP
.I /var/cache/hd-launcher
.TQ
.I ~/.cache/hd-launcher
The menu of the launcher and its icons, of the system and of a user.
.TP
.I /var/log/hyprland.log
The output of Hyprland.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/greetd.service.d/hd-desktop.conf
Runs
.B hd-desktop-render
before greetd starts, and runs the session on tty1, in the place of
.BR getty@tty1.service ,
after the boot splash is handed over to it.
.TP
.I /etc/dconf/db/local.d
.TQ
.I /etc/gtk-3.0/settings.ini
.TQ
.I /etc/gtk-4.0/settings.ini
The dark theme and the font of the GTK applications.
.TP
.I ~/.config/qt5ct/qt5ct.conf
.TQ
.I ~/.config/qt6ct/qt6ct.conf
.TQ
.I /usr/share/qt5ct/colors/hyprdebian.conf
.TQ
.I /usr/share/qt6ct/colors/hyprdebian.conf
The dark theme and the font of the Qt applications.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hypr/hyprtoolkit.conf
The colours and the font of the dialogs of Hyprland.
.SH SEE ALSO
